        "original_id": "7caa4a76-cedd-55d7-94a3-e6954893e65b",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: John Erskine, Earl of Mar; George I of Great Britain; James Francis Edward Stuart\n- Events: Jacobite rising of 1715; Battle of Sheriffmuir; Skirmish of Dunfermline\n- Places: Great Britain; Ireland; Scotland; Dunfermline; Hanover\n- Organizations: House of Stuart\n\nFacts:\n- George I of Great Britain, born George Louis, ruled the Duchy and Electorate of Brunswick-Lüneburg (Hanover) in the Holy Roman Empire from 1698.\n- George I of Great Britain was King of Great Britain and Ireland from 1 August 1714 until his death on 11 June 1727.\n- In 1714, the new king deprived John Erskine, Earl of Mar, of office.\n- After losing office in 1714, John Erskine, Earl of Mar, raised the standard of rebellion against the Hanoverians.\n- The Jacobite rising of 1715, also called the Fifteen or Lord Mar's Revolt, was an attempt by James Francis Edward Stuart to recover the thrones of England, Ireland, and Scotland for the exiled House of Stuart.\n- The Skirmish of Dunfermline took place in Dunfermline, Scotland, on 24 October 1715 as part of the Jacobite rising of 1715.\n- At the Battle of Sheriffmuir in November 1715, John Erskine, Earl of Mar, commanded a force larger than his opponent's, but he failed to win a victory.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
